//@ run-pass
//@ expect-stdout-contains: read_byte/read_line regression ok
//
// Regression gate for PR #2623 review comments (Copilot threads 4068240080,
// 4068240108, 4113839765, 4113946887, 4116322276) on the aarch64 emitters
// emit_read_byte_a64() / emit_read_line_a64() in
// self-hosted/compiler/lean_single.sio.
//
// Bug 1 (macOS): a failed `read()` syscall on aarch64-macos signals the error
// by setting the CARRY flag and returning a POSITIVE errno in x0 (XNU
// convention), whereas aarch64-linux returns a plain negative errno with no
// carry semantics (see the comment on emit_file_size_a64 in the same file,
// which already handled this correctly for open()). emit_read_byte_a64()/
// emit_read_line_a64() emitted a bare `cmp x0, #0` immediately after the
// read() syscall, which clobbers that carry flag and reads the positive
// errno as a "successful" byte count -- so on a genuine macOS read() failure,
// read_byte() returned an uninitialized stack byte instead of -1.
//
// Fix 1: normalize a carry-set result to a safe sentinel before the shared
// byte-count comparison, gated on `TARGET_OS == 2` (macOS):
//   - emit_read_byte_a64(): sentinel = -1 (this function's own EOF/error
//     contract; safe because the value is only ever compared/returned, never
//     used as a buffer index).
//   - emit_read_line_a64(): sentinel = 0, NOT -1. This function indexes the
//     mmap'd buffer at buf[bytes_read] on the EOF/error path; -1 would compute
//     buf[-1], a write one byte before the mapped page. 0 reuses the existing,
//     safe "empty line" EOF path.
//
// Bug 2 (Linux, thread 4113839765): aarch64-linux has no carry-flag error
// signaling -- a failed read() returns -errno directly in x0 (e.g. -5 for
// EIO). emit_read_line_a64()'s Linux path skipped the newline-strip probe on
// a non-positive result (correctly) but never clamped x0 before the
// null-terminate step below it also uses as a buffer index, so a genuine read
// failure computed buf[x0] with x0 negative -- e.g. buf[-5] -- an
// out-of-bounds WRITE before the mapped page (worse than Bug 1, which was
// only a wrong read). emit_read_byte_a64()'s Linux path has no such buffer
// index on its EOF/error branch (it just returns -1 unconditionally there),
// so it was not affected.
//
// Fix 2: on the Linux (`TARGET_OS != 2`) path of emit_read_line_a64(), clamp
// a negative x0 to 0 right after the read() syscall, before the shared
// EOF/error logic -- reusing the exact branch-based clamp idiom
// (`cmp`/`b.ge`/`movz #0`) this file already uses in emit_read_file_a64
// above (there on x2; here on x0). This matches the documented aarch64
// read_line() contract at docs/audit/windows_assert_a64_parity/READ_LINE.md
// section 3 ("clamp bytes_read < 0 -> 0" as a deliberate a64/x86 divergence).
//
// Bug 3 (thread 4113946887, upstream of Bugs 1 and 2): emit_read_line_a64()
// never checked its OWN allocating mmap() call for failure before using the
// result as "buf" -- pushing it, handing it to read() as the destination,
// and (via Bugs 1/2's own now-fixed clamps) eventually dereferencing it at
// the null-terminate `strb` near the end of the function. mmap() failing
// hits the exact same negative-errno (Linux) / carry-set-positive-errno
// (macOS) pattern as read() -- so an allocation failure that used to be
// silently miscompiled into a wrong read (Bugs 1/2) would, upstream of
// them, turn into an out-of-bounds write/segfault through a pointer that
// was never a valid buffer at all. emit_read_file_a64's own mmap() call has
// the same latent gap; it is out of scope here (not what the review flagged)
// and is tracked separately.
//
// Fix 3: check the mmap() result immediately after its syscall, before
// emit_push_a64() ever stores it as "buf" -- same OS-gated carry/negative
// check as Fixes 1/2, on failure jumping straight past the push/read/strip/
// null-terminate code to the function's normal return point, mirroring the
// unconditional-jump idiom (`let X = CL; em32(b); ...; patch_branch_a64(X,
// CL)`) emit_read_byte_a64's own EOF path already uses above.
//
// Bug 4 (thread 4116322276, follow-on from Fix 3): Fix 3's first cut set
// x0 = 0 (NULL) on the failure path, reasoning by analogy with read_byte()'s
// -1 int sentinel. That is not a usable empty string for a string-typed
// return -- it only *defers* the failure to the first string operation a
// caller performs on the result. Every string primitive in this file
// dereferences its argument unconditionally with no null check: e.g.
// emit_str_len_a64 just below does `ldrb w10, [x9, x0]` with x9 = the
// pointer, no guard. `str_len(read_line())` on the mmap-failure path would
// therefore fault reading address zero -- Bug 3's out-of-bounds write
// traded for an unconditional null dereference one call later, not fixed.
//
// Fix 4: point x0 at a freshly reserved, permanently zero-initialized
// 8-byte BSS slot instead of NULL (`bss_alloc_aligned(8)` +
// `emit_image_addr_a64`, the same allocation/address idiom
// RET_AGG_BSS_OFF and friends already use elsewhere in this file). BSS is
// zero-initialized by the loader, so the slot's first byte is already a
// valid NUL terminator with no store instruction needed, and -- unlike a
// stack address -- the pointer stays valid for the life of the process, so
// it is safe for a caller to hold onto. `bss_alloc_aligned` requires the
// `Div` effect (integer alignment division); emit_read_line_a64()'s own
// signature is widened from `with Mut, Panic` to `with Mut, Panic, Div` to
// permit the call (its sole caller, compile_primary_a64, already declares
// Div).
//
// This test exercises the three review-requested behaviours -- read_byte()
// returning the exact value 255 (0xFF) rather than confusing it with the -1
// sentinel, read_line() stripping a trailing '\n', and read_byte() returning a
// clean -1 at EOF -- by writing a small file and redirecting this process's own
// stdin (fd 0) to read from it via raw open()/dup2() syscalls, so the test does
// not depend on whatever stdin the test harness happens to provide.
//
// Scope note: this runs the DEFAULT (x86-64) engine, which is what CI's
// "Full Test Suite" job actually executes (souc-stage2 / lean_single native
// x86-64); it does not exercise either aarch64 fix at runtime (neither
// aarch64-linux nor aarch64-macos binaries execute in this repo's CI -- the
// macOS arm64 self-host job runs in `attest` mode only). The raw syscall
// numbers used below (open=2, dup2=33) are x86-64 Linux-specific, matching
// the existing convention in tests/run-pass/test_syscall_ffi.sio.
//
// All four fixes were verified by cross-compiling this exact probe shape
// (extended locally with a `str_len(read_line())` call, to exercise Fix 4's
// exact failure mode) for --target aarch64-linux and --target aarch64-macos
// and disassembling with an aarch64 objdump (aarch64-linux-gnu-objdump, or
// <triple>-objdump from any aarch64 binutils package -- e.g. Alpine's
// binutils-aarch64 ships aarch64-alpine-linux-musl-objdump), using a real
// x86-64 execution environment to run the seed compiler (Docker/Rosetta or,
// when that was unavailable mid-session, a local Lima/QEMU x86_64 VM --
// lean_single itself is an x86-64 Linux ELF and cannot run natively on an
// Apple Silicon host without one of those). Observed, byte for byte,
// read_line()'s mmap() call immediately followed by its read() call:
//   aarch64-macos:
//     svc #0x80 (mmap) -> b.cc <a> -> adrp/add x0,&empty -> b <end>  (Fix 4)
//     <a>: push buf; ...; svc #0x80 (read)
//          -> b.cc <b> -> mov x0,#0 -> <b>: cmp x0, #0x0             (Fix 1)
//   aarch64-linux:
//     svc #0x0 (mmap) -> cmp x0,#0 -> b.ge <a> -> movz/movk x0,&empty
//          -> b <end>                                                (Fix 4)
//     <a>: push buf; ...; svc #0x0 (read)
//          -> cmp x0,#0 -> b.ge <b> -> mov x0,#0 -> <b>: cmp x0, #0x0 (Fix 2)
// `&empty` is a non-zero, in-image address on both targets -- confirmed by
// hand-decoding the emitted movz/movk (Linux: 0x101006e0) and adrp/add
// (macOS: 0x1100000 + 0x6e0 = 0x11006e0) immediates -- and .bss grew by
// exactly 8 bytes relative to the Fix-1..3 build, matching the new
// `bss_alloc_aligned(8)` reservation. In both builds, <end> lands exactly on
// the instruction right after the function's normal `emit_pop_x0_a64()` --
// confirmed by disassembly address arithmetic, i.e. the mmap-failure path
// never executes the push or the pop and cannot corrupt the stack.
// read_byte() has no mmap() call and is unaffected by Fixes 3/4; its own
// fixup is unchanged (see Fix 1/Bug 2 above). The corrected build was also
// run natively on x86-64 with piped stdin ("hello world\n"): read_byte()
// consumed 'h' (prints "byte"), read_line() returned "ello world"
// (str_len 10, printed correctly) -- confirming the surrounding refactor
// (widening emit_read_line_a64 to `with Mut, Panic, Div` for the
// bss_alloc_aligned call) introduced no regression on the success path.
// To reproduce:
//   SOUNIO_SOUC_ENGINE=lean_single ./bin/souc compile \
//       tests/run-pass/a64_read_byte_read_line_carry_regression.sio \
//       -o /tmp/rb.a64.elf --target aarch64-macos   # or aarch64-linux
//   <aarch64-triple>-objdump -D -b binary -m aarch64 /tmp/rb.a64.elf | less

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Panic {
    // Craft a controlled stdin: a 0xFF byte, then "hello\n", then EOF.
    let path = "/tmp/sounio_a64_rb_regress_input.bin"
    let content = "\xFFhello\n"
    write_file(path, content, str_len(content))

    // open(path, O_RDONLY) then dup2(fd, 0): redirect this process's own
    // stdin to read from the file above instead of whatever the harness
    // gives us.
    let fd = syscall6(2, path, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    assert(fd > 0)
    let dup_rc = syscall6(33, fd, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    assert(dup_rc == 0)

    // (1) read_byte() must zero-extend 0xFF to 255, not confuse it with the
    // -1 EOF/error sentinel.
    let b1 = read_byte()
    assert(b1 == 255)

    // (2) read_line() must strip the trailing '\n'.
    let s1 = read_line()
    assert(str_len(s1) == 5)
    assert(str_eq(s1, "hello"))

    // (3) read_byte() must return -1 cleanly once stdin is exhausted (EOF),
    // not a garbage/uninitialized byte.
    let b2 = read_byte()
    assert(b2 == 0 - 1)

    syscall6(3, fd, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    print("read_byte/read_line regression ok\n")
    0
}
